<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  packer/win-server-2022/cd/Autounattend.xml.pkrtpl

  WHAT THIS IS
    The Windows Setup answer file for golden template 9002 (tpl-win-server-2022),
    rendered by Packer's templatefile() and written to the root of a small generated
    ISO labelled PACKERCD.

  WHY IT EXISTS
    Windows Setup is an interactive installer. Every question it would ask a human -
    which disk, which edition, which keyboard, what password - has to be answered
    here or the build sits at a dialog until Packer's winrm_timeout expires.

  HOW WINDOWS FINDS IT
    Setup scans the root of every removable, read-only volume for a file named
    exactly "Autounattend.xml". No floppy drive is involved; the proxmox-iso builder
    does not support floppies at all.

  THE FOUR PASSES USED BELOW, IN THE ORDER WINDOWS RUNS THEM
    windowsPE   - runs inside the boot environment, before anything is on disk.
                  This is the ONLY place VirtIO drivers can be injected early enough
                  to matter, and where the disk gets partitioned.
    specialize  - runs after the image is applied, before first boot completes.
                  Machine identity: computer name, time zone, RDP.
    oobeSystem  - runs at the out-of-box experience. Local Administrator password,
                  autologon, and the FirstLogonCommands that bootstrap WinRM so
                  Packer can finally connect.

  ⚠️ THE PASSWORD BELOW IS IN PLAINTEXT.
    That is how Windows answer files work; there is no way around it for a password
    Setup itself must consume. It is a build-only credential supplied from the
    environment, the answer file's ISO is unmounted before the template is created,
    C:\Windows\Panther is scrubbed in 90-cleanup.ps1, and sysprep /generalize clears
    the LSA secret. Rotate the real Administrator password from Ansible anyway.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend"
          xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
          xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- PASS 1: windowsPE                                                     -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <settings pass="windowsPE">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <SetupUILanguage>
        <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      </SetupUILanguage>
      <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
    </component>
-->

    <!--
      THE POINT OF THIS WHOLE TEMPLATE.

      Windows Setup draws its disk list from whatever storage controllers WinPE can
      already see. WinPE ships no VirtIO driver, so on a virtio-scsi controller it
      shows an empty list and the install dead-ends. The historical workaround - and
      what the old hand-built dc-01 actually did - was to fall back to a SATA disk
      and an E1000 NIC. That is emulated 1990s hardware carried forward forever.

      PnpCustomizationsWinPE loads drivers INTO WinPE before the disk list is drawn,
      so Setup sees the real controller and installs onto it. The template then
      matches every other VM in the lab: scsi0 on virtio-scsi-single, virtio NIC.

      Every plausible CD drive letter is listed for every driver directory because
      drive letters in an answer file are positional and there is no way to run a
      lookup script this early. Paths that do not exist are noted in setupact.log and
      skipped, so the redundancy is free.
    -->
    <!--
      NO PnpCustomizationsWinPE COMPONENT. This is deliberate and was expensive to
      learn, so the reasoning is recorded rather than the code.

      The obvious way to get VirtIO drivers into WinPE is
      Microsoft-Windows-PnpCustomizationsWinPE with a DriverPaths list. On this
      media (Windows Server 2022, SERVER_EVAL_x64FRE_en-us, March 2022) that
      component causes Setup to abort at "Setup is starting" with:

        Windows could not apply the Windows PE bootstrap setting specified in the
        unattend answer file.

      Everything the component needs was verified GOOD from a Shift+F10 shell in
      the failing WinPE itself:

        E: really is the virtio CD                         (dir E: -> vioscsi, NetKVM, ...)
        the paths really exist                             (E:\vioscsi\2k22\amd64)
        the driver really loads                            (drvload -> "Successfully loaded")
        the disk really appears once it has                (wmic diskdrive get size -> 64420392960)

      So the drivers, the path and the drive letter were all correct and the
      component still refused to apply. Narrowing DriverPaths from sixteen
      speculative entries to four correct ones did not help either; removing the
      component was what let Setup proceed.

      RunSynchronous below does the same job by calling the drvload that was
      proven to work by hand. It runs at the start of windowsPE, before
      DiskConfiguration needs a disk to partition.
    -->

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">

      <!--
        GPT layout, because this VM boots OVMF/UEFI.

        An MBR DiskConfiguration will not boot under OVMF. The required shape is:
          1. EFI System Partition, FAT32, 260 MB  (the firmware reads this)
          2. Microsoft Reserved (MSR), 16 MB      (no filesystem, no letter)
          3. Windows, NTFS, everything else       (InstallTo points here)

        Note InstallTo PartitionID = 3, not 1. Getting that wrong installs Windows
        onto the 260 MB EFI partition and fails in a confusing way.

        WillWipeDisk = true is safe here and only here: this is a fresh Packer VM
        with one empty disk.
      -->
      <!--
        Load the VirtIO drivers before anything needs a disk.

        Order matters: RunSynchronous executes at the start of windowsPE, so the
        storage driver is present by the time DiskConfiguration below looks for
        DiskID 0. Without it WinPE sees no disks and the install dead-ends - which
        is exactly why the old hand-built dc-01 fell back to SATA and E1000.

        The command SEARCHES for the driver rather than assuming a drive letter.
        A first attempt hardcoded E:, which is where the virtio CD genuinely ends
        up once Setup is running - verified from a shell in WinPE. It still did
        not work, because RunSynchronous executes before optical drives are
        reliably lettered, so E: did not resolve yet at that moment.

        `if exist` makes a non-matching letter a no-op rather than an error, which
        is safe here in a way that listing many paths in PnpCustomizationsWinPE
        was NOT - there, one path that failed to resolve failed the entire pass.
      -->
      <RunSynchronous>
%{ for i, d in driver_dirs ~}
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>${i + 1}</Order>
          <Path>cmd /c for %d in (D E F G H I) do @if exist %d:\${d}\${inf_names[i]} drvload %d:\${d}\${inf_names[i]}</Path>
          <Description>Load VirtIO ${d}</Description>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
%{ endfor ~}
      </RunSynchronous>

      <DiskConfiguration>
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <Type>EFI</Type>
              <Size>260</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <Type>MSR</Type>
              <Size>16</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <Type>Primary</Type>
              <Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <PartitionID>1</PartitionID>
              <Label>System</Label>
              <Format>FAT32</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
              <Label>Windows</Label>
              <Letter>C</Letter>
              <Format>NTFS</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <!--
            Which edition to install. See the windows_image_index variable for how to
            confirm the index on YOUR ISO:
              dism /Get-WimInfo /WimFile:D:\sources\install.wim
          -->
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/INDEX</Key>
              <Value>${image_index}</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo>
            <DiskID>0</DiskID>
            <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
          </InstallTo>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <UserData>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
        <FullName>MutaSpace SOC Lab</FullName>
        <Organization>MutaSpace</Organization>
%{ if product_key != "" ~}
        <ProductKey>
          <Key>${product_key}</Key>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </ProductKey>
%{ else ~}
        <!--
          No ProductKey element at all. Evaluation media carries its own edition and
          rejects a key. If you are installing from Volume Licensing media instead,
          set the product_key variable to the appropriate GVLK.
        -->
%{ endif ~}
      </UserData>

      <EnableFirewall>true</EnableFirewall>
      <EnableNetwork>true</EnableNetwork>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- PASS 2: specialize                                                    -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <settings pass="specialize">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <!--
        A build-time placeholder name, NOT dc-01.

        This is a generalised template. The real computer name is set per-VM after
        cloning - by Cloudbase-Init from the OpenTofu initialization block, and then
        authoritatively by Ansible when microsoft.ad promotes the machine. Baking
        "dc-01" in here would give every clone of this template the same name.
      -->
      <ComputerName>${computer_name}</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>UTC</TimeZone>
    </component>

    <!--
      RDP on. This is a lab whose whole purpose is that learners look at things, and
      the Windows console through the Proxmox noVNC viewer is painful. UserAuthentication
      0 disables Network Level Authentication, which is a real weakening - it is
      accepted here because the machine sits behind fw-01 on an isolated lab LAN, and
      because turning NLA back on is itself a worthwhile hardening exercise.
    -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-TerminalServices-LocalSessionManager"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <fDenyTSConnections>false</fDenyTSConnections>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-TerminalServices-RDP-WinStationExtensions"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <UserAuthentication>0</UserAuthentication>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- PASS 3: oobeSystem                                                    -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <settings pass="oobeSystem">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">

      <!--
        Every OOBE screen a human would click through.

        ProtectYourPC = 3 declines the optional telemetry/privacy defaults rather than
        accepting them. SkipMachineOOBE and SkipUserOOBE are deliberately NOT used:
        they have been deprecated for years and cause more breakage on current builds
        than the explicit Hide* settings they were meant to replace.
      -->
      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
      </OOBE>

      <!--
        Setting AdministratorPassword enables the built-in Administrator account and
        is what stops OOBE from demanding that a new user be created.
      -->
      <UserAccounts>
        <AdministratorPassword>
          <Value>${admin_password}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </AdministratorPassword>
      </UserAccounts>

      <!--
        Autologon exists for exactly one reason: FirstLogonCommands do not run until
        somebody logs on, and the thing they run is the WinRM bootstrap that lets
        Packer connect at all.

        LogonCount 2 leaves one spare logon in case a driver install forces a reboot
        before the bootstrap finishes. The AutoAdminLogon and DefaultPassword registry
        values this creates are deleted in scripts/90-cleanup.ps1 - note that the
        commonly-cited "AutoLogonCount = 0" trick is a logon-counter fix and does NOT
        scrub those values.
      -->
      <AutoLogon>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <Username>Administrator</Username>
        <LogonCount>2</LogonCount>
        <Password>
          <Value>${admin_password}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
      </AutoLogon>

      <TimeZone>UTC</TimeZone>

      <!--
        Find setup.ps1 by VOLUME LABEL, never by drive letter.

        Three CDs are attached during the build. Adding, removing or reordering one
        shifts every letter after it. The label is stable; D:/E:/F: are not.

        The command deliberately contains no & < or > characters - those are XML
        metacharacters and would have to be entity-escaped, which is a classic source
        of answer files that parse but do nothing. Dot-sourcing runs the script in the
        current session without needing the call operator.
      -->
      <FirstLogonCommands>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Bootstrap WinRM and the QEMU guest agent for Packer</Description>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "$v = Get-Volume | Where-Object { $_.FileSystemLabel -eq '${seed_cd_label}' } | Select-Object -First 1; . ($v.DriveLetter + ':\setup.ps1')"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
      </FirstLogonCommands>
    </component>
  </settings>
</unattend>
